@page "/tabs"

<CatHead Title="Tabs">
    <code>.tabs</code> swaps a region of <em>content</em> in place. A strip that changes a
    <em>setting</em> is the <a href="/segmented">segmented control</a>, and a strip of links to
    other addresses only looks like tabs &mdash; the last example.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-warn">
    <i class="ri-error-warning-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The classes cannot supply the ARIA</span>
        Tabs need <code>role="tablist"</code>,
        <code>role="tab"</code>, <code>aria-selected</code> and <code>aria-controls</code>, plus
        arrow-key movement between them &mdash; <code>data-tabs</code> supplies the movement, and the
        markup supplies the rest.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Tabs.Tabs" Title="Tabs" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>The demo is live.</strong> <code>data-tabs</code> on the container hands the strip
        to <code>sednaUi.tabs</code> — clicking, arrow keys, <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd>, showing
        the matching <code>aria-controls</code> panel, and the roving <code>tabindex</code> that
        makes <kbd>Tab</kbd> step over the whole tablist. Mark the selected tab with
        <code>aria-selected="true"</code> rather than <code>.tab--active</code>, which is for a
        strip that is genuinely not a tablist. An app driving its tabs from C# leaves
        <code>data-tabs</code> off — otherwise two things set <code>aria-selected</code> — and wires
        the arrow keys in the component.
    </p>
</CatExample>



<CatExample TExample="Examples.Tabs.TabsInACard" Title="Tabs in a card" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — switch between them.</strong> The tablist goes in
        <a href="/card"><code>.card-head</code></a> and the panels in the body.
        <strong>Add <code>.card-head--tabs</code> to the head:</strong> it hands the head's padding
        to the strip, so the head's own border becomes the strip's line and the active tab's
        indicator meets the body edge — a plain <code>.card-head</code> draws two lines and anchors
        the indicator to nothing.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Tabs.LinkStrip" Title="A strip of links" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same look over a row of <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>s to other addresses &mdash; a record's
        sections, each a page of its own. This is not a tablist: there is no panel to swap and no
        arrow-key contract, so it is a <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> with an <code>aria-label</code>,
        <code>.tab--active</code> marks the current page and <code>aria-current="page"</code> is
        what announces it, as <code>ActiveLink</code> writes both. Leave <code>role</code> and
        <code>data-tabs</code> off.
    </p>
</CatExample>
